# Learn answer set programming with Elixir

## Introduction

Nineteen guided lessons introduce answer set programming (ASP), constraint
modeling, search, and the Elixir wrapper. Clingo grounds and solves the ASP;
Elixir supplies inputs and presents copied results in tables.

Start with **Facts, rules and alternatives** if ASP is new to you. Choose
**Getting started with clingo_elixir** if you already know ASP. Each lesson
contains its own setup and can be opened independently.

## Open your first notebook

1. Keep this repository on the same machine as your Livebook runtime. Use
   **Elixir 1.20.x and OTP 29+**, both 64-bit, on a
   [platform with an available bundle](../README.md#platforms-and-installation).
   Clingo and the NIF are bundled; no separate solver or compiler is needed.
2. If Livebook is not installed, install the version used to validate the course:

   ```sh
   mix escript.install hex livebook 0.19.9 --force
   ```

3. Open a terminal in the `clingo_elixir` project folder and start Livebook:

   ```sh
   ~/.mix/escripts/livebook server livebooks/intro.livemd
   ```

   On Windows, use PowerShell:

   ```powershell
   & "$HOME\.mix\escripts\livebook.bat" server livebooks/intro.livemd
   ```

   Open the local URL printed in the terminal if your browser does not open
   automatically. Keep that terminal running while using the notebooks.
4. Open [Facts, rules and alternatives](language/01-facts-and-rules.livemd) or
   [Getting started with clingo_elixir](elixir/01-getting-started.livemd).
   Use Livebook's default **Standalone** runtime and choose **Evaluate all**.
5. Wait for dependency setup to finish. It installs this checkout and Kino
   0.19.0; the first Kino installation needs network access. The
   **Preliminaries** cell then runs a real clingo query. A **satisfiable**
   table containing `ready` confirms that setup worked.

Keep notebooks in their repository folders so setup can find the wrapper,
presentation helper, and lesson inputs. No project-path environment variable
is needed. The default Standalone runtime must use the Elixir/OTP versions
above. When using a remote runtime, make the whole checkout available there.

## Choose a route

### Learn ASP

Start with [facts and rules](language/01-facts-and-rules.livemd), then continue
through [negation](language/02-negation-and-recursion.livemd),
[choices and constraints](language/03-choices-and-constraints.livemd),
[grounding and arithmetic](language/04-domains-and-grounding.livemd),
[aggregates](language/05-aggregates-and-conditionals.livemd), and
[optimization](language/06-optimization-and-projection.livemd).

### Model a problem

Explore [graphs and sets](modeling/01-graphs-and-sets.livemd),
[grids and puzzles](modeling/02-grids-and-puzzles.livemd),
[learning and configuration](modeling/03-learning-and-configuration.livemd),
[planning](planning/01-actions-and-robots.livemd), and
[scheduling](planning/02-scheduling-and-resources.livemd).

### Use the Elixir API

Read [Getting started](elixir/01-getting-started.livemd), then work with
[inputs, results, and streams](elixir/02-inputs-results-and-streams.livemd),
[incremental controls](elixir/03-incremental-solving.livemd), and
[inspection and export](elixir/04-inspection-and-export.livemd).

The [course contents](README.md) links every lesson, including the advanced
solver extensions. The [concept and API index](CONCEPTS.md) helps you find a
particular operation.

## Work through a lesson

Read the explanation, evaluate its cell, and inspect the returned models. Edit
ASP inside an Elixir string and reevaluate that cell and its dependants. The
**Evaluate all** action also works when starting a lesson again. Worked-answer
checks describe the original input; update them when an edit changes the answer.

Each ordinary solve closes its native control automatically. Incremental
examples use scoped controls within one cell. File-based sections create
editable copies of their inputs: run their workspace cell first, download
changes you want to keep, then evaluate the cleanup cell. Rerunning the workspace
cell gives you a fresh copy. Gated exercise cells stay disabled until you choose
to run your completed encoding.

If you update the checkout or replace a native bundle, restart the notebook
runtime before evaluating again. Clingo remains loaded for that runtime's
lifetime. Setup, inputs, solving, and presentation all stay local after the
initial dependency installation.
